.TH cc 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
cc \- the C compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cc
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-k
] [
.B \-r
] [
.BI \-o " out"
] [
.BI \-L dir
]
.IR file.c " | " file.bas
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cc
compiles C on the machine itself.  From
.B prog.c
it produces
.BR prog.bc ,
which is a program in its own right:
.BR ./prog.bc .
.PP
Given a
.B .bas
file it runs
.BR mmbc (1)
first and compiles the C that comes out, so a BASIC program can be built
in one step.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " out"
Name the output.  The default is the source name with
.B .bc
in place of its extension, in the current directory.
.TP
.B \-v
Show each pass as it runs.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the intermediates - the preprocessed source, the tokens, the IR,
and the C that
.B mmbc
generated from a BASIC file.
.TP
.B \-r
Run it.  When every pass has succeeded,
.B cc
execs the program it just built; if any pass failed, nothing runs.  This
is what
.BR mmedit (1)
uses for F2.
.TP
.BI \-L dir
Where the passes and their headers live.  The default is
.BR /usr/lib/cc/ .
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT TAKE
There is no
.BR \-c ,
.B \-S
or
.BR \-E :
one source file makes one program, and this target has neither an
assembler nor a linker.  There is no
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-l ,
.B \-M
or
.B \-X
either.  To see preprocessed source, run
.BR cpp (1)
by hand.
.SH WHAT IT PRODUCES
Bytecode with spans of native ARM Thumb in it, run by
.BR bcrun (1).
Headers live in
.BR /usr/lib/cc/include ,
and describe what
.B bcrun
provides rather than what the FUZIX libc does.  The library is resolved
by name at load time.
.SH LIMITS WORTH KNOWING
The compiler is small.  Deeply nested expressions and very large
functions can exceed it.
.PP
It also has a threshold past which a function stops being turned into
native ARM and falls back to bytecode, which is worth two to three times
on a hot loop.  If a small change makes a program suddenly slower, that
is the first thing to suspect;
.B BCRUN_BYTECODE=1
forces bytecode everywhere so the two can be compared.
.PP
The preprocessor is the board's own, not
.BR "gcc \-E" .
Expect C89 with some of C99: 160 of 175 conformance tests pass here.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# cc hello.c
# ./hello.bc
# cc -o prog.bc prog.c
# cc prog.bas
# cc -r prog.bas          build it and run it
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcrun (1),
.BR bcdump (1),
.BR mmbc (1),
.BR mmedit (1),
.BR cpp (1)
